  "- [ ] `domain-ir-boundaries` Domain IR registration and transition boundaries\n\nStatus: Open\n\nProjected task block:\nOwner role: compiler domain-IR interface maintainer. All tasks open; acceptance\nrequires actual registered entry, verifier and exit calls, not registry-shaped data.\n\n| Task | Concrete closing evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| domain-entry | Source compiler/c12/domain-entry: Build domain IR through declared entry passes recording consumed, preserved, invalidated and translated source/IR facts. Concrete acceptance: Mutate each consumed/preserved/invalidated/translated fact edge or remove its source/core/MIR anchor; the entry verifier must reject or regenerate the affected fact before the domain result is consumed. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. |\n| domain-fallback | Source compiler/c12/domain-fallback: For unsupported targets expose semantically legal fallback, residual MIR or explicit rejection with diagnostic and eligibility records. Concrete acceptance: Select an unsupported target with no fallback, a fallback that violates profile/effect/safety semantics, and an undeclared lowering; require C12-FALLBACK or C12-LOWERING with eligibility records, never fabricated executable success. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. |\n| domain-proof-boundary | Source compiler/c12/domain-proof-boundary: Expose optimized domain results only with proof/certificate/translation-validation or accepted nonsemantic evidence; reject benchmark/search/provider claims as sole correctness evidence. Concrete acceptance: Supply benchmark results, optimizer search output or a provider assertion in place of required checked proof/certificate/translation evidence; reject with C12-PROOF. Nonsemantic conformance acceptance must not authorize semantic change. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. |\n| domain-registration | Source compiler/c12/domain-registration: Register stable package-visible domain interfaces with owning schema/verifier/passes/profiles/lowerings/proof/fallback/diagnostic contracts, including plugin-provided domains. Concrete acceptance: Require EFIR registration and verification, schema IR registration and verification, workflow IR registration and verification, and registration and verification for one identified target-oriented domain IR (HDL/state-machine IR). Retain each family's package-visible registration, semantic-anchor map, verifier identity, actual invocation input/output and verdict. Reject a missing required family, an unverified family, a failed verifier or an untargeted registration substituted for the target-oriented case; one successful family cannot complete this population. Consume math, schema, workflow and backend-hdl producer interfaces for their corresponding family fixtures, not whole-component completion. Try absent schema/verifier/owner/anchor/entry/exit/profile/target/proof/fallback/diagnostic fields, unstable or conflicting package-visible identity and unauthorized opaque plugin payloads; reject registration before target use. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. |\n| registered-domain-exit | Source compiler/c12/registered-domain-exit: Permit domain exit/target consumption only via registered paths after verifier acceptance and accepted lowering/equivalence evidence. Concrete acceptance: Submit a failed verifier result, missing exact lowering/equivalence evidence and a path absent from the registration; reject target consumption before emitting an artifact. Retain actual input/output and diagnostic identities plus positive and rejected-case artifacts; supplied success flags, proposal delivery and broad parent links are not executable evidence. |\n\nYes/no acceptance: do registered entry/verifier/exit operations execute with\nexact anchors and complete facts? Do all invalid registrations, missing proofs,\nillegal exits and unavailable-target fallback cases reject as specified? Both\nmust be yes. Domain owners retain their payload schemas and semantics.\n\nDecision allocation interface/compiler/c12/domain-semantic-anchor (Open). Role: compiler domain-IR interface maintainer. Anchor domain artifacts to source syntax and typed core or MIR while preserving all downstream semantic/authority/safety/provenance facts under the owning payload schema. Refusals and conditional cases: Reject absent source syntax or typed-core/MIR anchors, a foreign or invalid owning payload schema, or loss of downstream type/effect/ownership/capability/profile/target/safety/provenance facts before domain consumption. Required outputs: Source-to-domain semantic anchor map, owning-schema validation and downstream fact-preservation report with C12-ANCHOR/SCHEMA/FACTS diagnostics. Consume actual dependency interface outputs: mir/current-evidence-and-remaining-work/mir3-verify-graph-and-legality; whole-component completion is not a prerequisite. Retain exact source/compiler/pass/profile/target and checker/provider identities, actual positive products and refused-case diagnostics. Implementation acceptance remains unassessed.\n\nC12 domain record, transition, verifier and diagnostic criterion (Open): Treat domain IRs as compiler artifacts refining typed Core or MIR semantics, with source syntax and semantic anchors, registered pass contracts and target paths; they do not create separate untyped languages. Register the actual domain identity, owning document/schema, semantic anchor, entry/exit passes, verifier, supported profiles, target lowerings, optimization rules, proof obligations, fallback policy and diagnostics, with stable package-visible identities for plugin/library registration and self-hosting consumers. Registration examples for EFIR, pass names, profiles, targets and provider fallback illustrate shape and confer no universal support or accepted proof. Bind every domain artifact's domain/artifact identity, source syntax/span/origin chain, typed-core or MIR anchors, active profile/target request, required type/effect/ownership/capability/profile/target/safety/provenance facts, actual verifier identity/result, proof references and lowering status; its payload uses the owning domain schema. Example workflow values, empty proofs and accepted/eligible labels do not substitute for actual verification or lowering evidence. Entry families include expanded source forms, typed-core subgraphs, MIR subgraphs, provider declarations, package manifests, schema declarations and compiler plugin output; record consumed/preserved/invalidated/translated facts. Exit families include MIR subgraphs, target provider calls, backend IR, runtime manifests, generated code, package artifacts and verification artifacts. Each exit waits for actual domain verifier acceptance and equivalence/proof/certificate/accepted-lowering evidence through its registered path. Verify schema, semantic anchors, source provenance, type/effect/capability/profile compatibility, owning domain invariants, safety/proof obligations, target preconditions, fallback legality and diagnostic completeness; failed verification prevents emission for that artifact. Specialized optimization uses proof replay, certificate validation, translation validation against MIR/typed core, conformance acceptance for nonsemantic choices or explicit rejection with diagnostics; nonsemantic fixtures never authorize a semantic rewrite, and search/benchmark/provider claims alone prove no correctness. Baseline families retain EFIR with adjacent EML proof/search, schema, workflow graph, AI agent/tool manifest, HDL/state-machine, GPU kernel, query, FFI boundary and package/artifact graph IR; each owning phase defines its payload, and enumerating a family grants no implementation support. Required products are the registry, domain artifact schemas, source-to-domain anchor map, entry/exit records, verifier report, proof/certificate references, target-lowering eligibility matrix, fallback/rejection records and diagnostics. Preserve all nine diagnostic meanings: C12-REGISTRATION malformed registration, C12-ANCHOR invalid/missing anchor, C12-SCHEMA invalid payload schema, C12-FACTS lost semantic/authority/safety facts, C12-VERIFY failed verifier, C12-PROOF missing accepted optimization evidence, C12-LOWERING unsupported lowering, C12-FALLBACK missing/illegal fallback and C12-PLUGIN plugin IR outside policy. Include domain/artifact identity, source span, semantic anchor, owner document, profile, target, verifier, missing fact and remediation. Actual future positive/refused conformance covers every required record and verifier field, all consumed/produced transition families claimed, source-through-MIR anchors, accepted proof/certificate optimization and rejection without evidence, target eligibility/fallback, plugin policy and the named diagnostic cases, retaining the existing EFIR/schema/workflow/identified HDL registration population. Consume authentic C11, MATH3/MATH4, owning domain, SAFE15, PERF1/PERF10 and backend interfaces without accepting their full implementations. Preserve all existing rows, outcome IDs, statuses and unresolved compiler ordering. This source amendment runs no entry/verifier/exit callback, optimizer, compiler, target, runtime/provider, proof checker or held operation and confers no implementation, release, migration or final-count acceptance.\n\n\n<!-- compiler-api-facet-source-ir:start -->\n\nApply the [complete facet source criteria](../expand-workflow/plan.md#complete-facet-source-criteria) within this existing Open task. Preserve original criteria, historical review scopes and actual restrictions; current source/interface and implementation acceptance remain pending.\n\nL14 complete facet IR-carrier criterion (Open): Verify actual facet id/version, source-span map, generated-origin map, type/effect annotations, profile/target assumptions, capability requirements, build inputs/generated outputs, validation results/proof obligations, stable serialization and compatibility version at declared entry/exit boundaries. Bind actual owning payload schema/verifier and checked Core/MIR semantic anchors under C12; pure Core-only facets need no invented domain IR. Structured external syntax retains original-source diagnostics. Consumers preserve enough source mapping, privacy, fact and authority evidence, invalidating and regenerating changed subjects before reuse. Reject absent/stale/incompatible/opaque/unversioned payloads, lost anchors or privacy fields and failed owner verification before emission. Required evidence: real source/IR/serialization/consumer products and missing-field, changed-subject and invalid-output refusals; a schema/status flag is not verifier execution.\n<!-- compiler-api-facet-source-ir:end -->\n\n\n\n<!-- compiler-api-facet-source-families:start -->\n\nApply the [complete facet source criteria](../expand-workflow/plan.md#complete-facet-source-criteria) within this existing Open task. Preserve original criteria, historical review scopes and actual restrictions; current source/interface and implementation acceptance remain pending.\n\nL14 complete initial-facet composition criterion (Open): Keep all ten initial schema, efir, zk, hardware, workflow, agent, ui, query, compiler and ffi facets visible with their actual existing producer/checker tasks, versioned payloads and declared entries/exits. EFIR carries math semantics while EML serves proof/search; zk exposes relation, public/private split, witness/credential custody, disclosure policy, provider trust and verifier-artifact shape. Schema/Math/Workflow/AI/UI/Query, Crypto/Formal, Platform/HDL, compiler/plugin and Runtime/ABI owners retain their real semantics; this task registers and composes rather than duplicating them. Required unavailable owners remain explicit future work, and one initial demonstration does not discharge all families. Reject missing/foreign owners, opaque proof/private-computation calls and claimed support from a registry inventory alone. Required evidence: actual per-facet owner/schema/checker/transition joins plus selected accepted/refused products; no product ISA, live provider or cryptographic result is invented.\n<!-- compiler-api-facet-source-families:end -->",
